<pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I576935622128119dafe7660841025ec76e7ec6d6" commit="f0db97eda0bb655da57a6f7c8a503567188e73f1" commit_message="Clean up coreboot-utils and change it to be pulled in by userfeedback  sys-apps/coreboot-utils serves two purposes: with cros_host it installs several utilities that are needed to build a firmware image, and for an actual board it installs the cbmem utility that is used to query runtime firmware information (e.g. for chrome://system). (It also installs cbfstool for a factory use case that should probably be solved by supplying it externally instead, but that is out of scope for this bug... see crosbug.com/p/49634.)  However, the dependencies that pull coreboot-utils in are very weird: the cros_host use case is solved by build-time DEPENDs from the various firmware ebuilds, which works fine... but on base images it seems that it only gets installed as an RDEPEND of the depthcharge ebuild that should've really been a DEPEND anyway. The problem is that the depthcharge ebuild doesn't always get emerged on system images, because it depends on the USE=bootimage flag. This is only set for a few boards that we explicitly selected to continue building ToT firmware for the CQ, whereas the actual firmware running on Chromebooks comes from firmware branches. (The chipset-skylake-private overlay also has a custom dependency on coreboot-utils, presumably to get it to appear on some boards that didn't have USE=bootimage set on ToT.)  This patch fixes this weirdness by pulling coreboot-utils in from the userfeedback ebuild instead, which contains the code that actually calls these utilities. Also add it unconditionally to developer images just in case. Fix up depthcharge's dependencies and make a few of the installed utilities conditional to cros_host which aren't really needed outside the build process.  Removing the sys-apps/pciutils dependency because I have no idea how it got there in the first place. I don't know of anything in coreboot-utils that would require it at runtime. If people want it available for testing purposes, it still gets pulled in directly by chromium-os-test.  Removing the old USE=coreboot flag from all remaining packages while I'm here... this flag was only used to differentiate coreboot systems from legacy EFI firmware many years ago, and has become vestigal with recent firmware build refactorings.  BUG=b:37757150 TEST=None  Change-Id: I576935622128119dafe7660841025ec76e7ec6d6 Signed-off-by: Julius Werner &lt;jwerner@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/495366 " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="495366" owner_email="jwerner@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="5" project="chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/overlays/chromiumos-overlay" ref="refs/changes/66/495366/5" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Ibc0584f4e9a5ed6191d12f89816e1dd5c47908dd" commit="cd853cc779f9e99dafd4684717b04cec53ed4f30" commit_message="power: suspend_stress_test: Read firmware log through cbmem -1  We do not want firmware errors from a previous boot (with a possibly different firmware version) to show up in the suspend stress test. Therefore, switch from reading /sys/firmware/log to calling the cbmem utility which knows how to cut the log off at the latest boot.  CQ-DEPEND=CL:495366,CL:494606 BUG=b:37757150 TEST=None  Change-Id: Ibc0584f4e9a5ed6191d12f89816e1dd5c47908dd Signed-off-by: Julius Werner &lt;jwerner@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/495626 Reviewed-by: Furquan Shaikh &lt;furquan@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="495626" owner_email="jwerner@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/platform2" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform2" ref="refs/changes/26/495626/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I2b5076d21bcd0f8d17fd811cad2ff7bd200b112a" commit="887d96e6b5675f04be4188724a6cc5cbbe292952" commit_message="npcx: i2c: Add support for npcx7 series ec  This CL added support for 8 i2c controllers and 11 i2c ports in npcx7 series ec. we also added i2c-npcx5/7.c and moved the functions related to chip family to them. (Such as i2c_port_to_controller(), i2c_select_port() and so on.) Note the layout and bit position of i2c registers which are accessed in these functions are irregular between npcx5 and npcx7. We think abstracting them from i2c.c is easier to maintain.  In this CL, we also modified the checking rule for I2C_PORT_COUNT in task.h in order to prevent compiler error. So far, the ECs besides stm32 only use TASK_EVENT_I2C_IDLE to wait for i2c hardware completes its job. Put (I2C_PORT_COUNT &gt; TASK_EVENT_MAX_I2C) checking rule for all ECs seems not suitable.  It also includes 1. Remove useless NPCX_I2C_PUBIT macro function. 2. Remove useless NPCX_PWDWN_CTL_COUNT in registers.h. 3. Add CGC_OFFSET_I2C2 and CGC_I2C_MASK2 to power down the other 4 i2c    controllers of npcx7 ec.  BRANCH=none BUG=none TEST=No build errors for all boards using npcx5 series.      Build poppy board and upload FW to platform. No issues found.      All 8 i2c controllers and 10 ports (npcx796f supports PSL.) passed      i2c stress tests on npcx796f evb.  Change-Id: I2b5076d21bcd0f8d17fd811cad2ff7bd200b112a Signed-off-by: Mulin Chao &lt;mlchao@nuvoton.com&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/487541 Reviewed-by: Randall Spangler &lt;rspangler@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="487541" owner_email="mlchao@nuvoton.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="4" project="chromiumos/platform/ec" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec" ref="refs/changes/41/487541/4" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I7c6bdd64e6633a0e36626c8c34e688a891aed024" commit="102ef6bdeeb94cfdb8368fcc33860453d0130472" commit_message="eclass: import epunt-cxx.eclass from upstream Gentoo  This CL imports epunt-cxx.eclass (at commit 71791dbb2b3c55e8b99b769a5f86f377df594a46) from upstream Gentoo.  BUG=chromium:680616 TEST=Remote trybot runs.  Change-Id: I7c6bdd64e6633a0e36626c8c34e688a891aed024 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/493551 Commit-Ready: Ben Chan &lt;benchan@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Ben Chan &lt;benchan@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Mike Frysinger &lt;vapier@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="1" gerrit_number="493551" owner_email="benchan@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/overlays/portage-stable" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/overlays/portage-stable" ref="refs/changes/51/493551/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="1"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I7dc1cb5f16a8c772ea2e38a47066175a97218ac8" commit="78bfb8c5de8c61ca70a0c324b718e370e7cb9c4e" commit_message="Fix errors in camera_V4L2  1. REQBUFS ioctl should be after munmap. Otherwise, device is busy. 2. Frame rate should be float. Some devices may support (2/15) frame rate.  BUG=chromium:718278 TEST=test on nyan_big and pass the test.  Change-Id: I7dc1cb5f16a8c772ea2e38a47066175a97218ac8 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/495947 Commit-Ready: Heng-ruey Hsu &lt;henryhsu@google.com&gt; Tested-by: Heng-ruey Hsu &lt;henryhsu@google.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Hung-yu Wu &lt;hywu@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="495947" owner_email="henryhsu@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/third_party/autotest" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/autotest" ref="refs/changes/47/495947/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I39ac4813a8a2b9d9c32e3431b6d720a0d9f5ce56" commit="542429e909a885c218f6821e0d5ce0d39251f825" commit_message="Remove pd check.  Change-Id: I39ac4813a8a2b9d9c32e3431b6d720a0d9f5ce56 Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/490496 Commit-Ready: danny chan &lt;dchan@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: danny chan &lt;dchan@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Tyler Reid &lt;twreid@google.com&gt; " fail_count="3" gerrit_number="490496" owner_email="dchan@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="4" project="chromiumos/third_party/autotest" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/autotest" ref="refs/changes/96/490496/4" remote="cros" total_fail_count="3"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I281497cc6fe251d48c4ea737c7b4242200d39421" commit="96b8d55b52699f87492502aa6f54f11217392c3b" commit_message="eve: Enable TCPC low power mode  This mode had to be disabled for P1 because of leakages on PP3300_A that affected the voltage levels on the CC lines. With EVT the leakage issues have been resolved and now this feature can be enabled.  BUG=b:35648532 BRANCH=none TEST=With lidopen attached sink only adapter to port 1. Then executed 'lidclose' via the EC console and verified that the port went into low power mode as expected. In addition, without adapter connected, verified that when in low power mode the EC console was not flooded with low power messages.  Change-Id: I281497cc6fe251d48c4ea737c7b4242200d39421 Signed-off-by: Scott Collyer &lt;scollyer@google.com&gt; Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/496947 Commit-Ready: Scott Collyer &lt;scollyer@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Scott Collyer &lt;scollyer@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Duncan Laurie &lt;dlaurie@google.com&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="496947" owner_email="scollyer@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/platform/ec" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/platform/ec" ref="refs/changes/47/496947/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="Ifce9d359d0067c68a48bc6bdd9605c15aaba084e" commit="9575694e6fded9f763865bb20912f642981f0ccf" commit_message="cbuildbot: Add gonzo board to build gonzo images.  BUG=b:37908621 TEST=TODO  Change-Id: Ifce9d359d0067c68a48bc6bdd9605c15aaba084e Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/494067 Commit-Ready: Harsh Modi &lt;harshmodi@google.com&gt; Tested-by: Harsh Modi &lt;harshmodi@google.com&gt; Reviewed-by: Don Garrett &lt;dgarrett@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="494067" owner_email="harshmodi@google.com" pass_count="0" patch_number="1" project="chromiumos/chromite" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/chromite" ref="refs/changes/67/494067/1" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><pending_commit branch="master" change_id="I7a2724ec4df551eb2fa4b4b3f01a577c287ca21d" commit="5fb8a400e542b3ff4bae3ef7d588dbf9dd18c033" commit_message="security_SandboxedServices: Don't fail when reducing privilege.  Temporarily prevent the test from failing when we're switching a service to not run as root.  We're having trouble with dependent Autotest CLs so allow reducing privilege for a service without the test failing. Once the Autotest situation stabilizes, we can revert this CL.  BUG=chromium:715678,chromium:716151 TEST=Passes with https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/488701/ TEST=without updating the baseline.  Change-Id: I7a2724ec4df551eb2fa4b4b3f01a577c287ca21d Reviewed-on: https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/494007 Commit-Ready: Jorge Lucangeli Obes &lt;jorgelo@chromium.org&gt; Tested-by: Jorge Lucangeli Obes &lt;jorgelo@chromium.org&gt; Reviewed-by: Jorge Lucangeli Obes &lt;jorgelo@chromium.org&gt; " fail_count="0" gerrit_number="494007" owner_email="jorgelo@chromium.org" pass_count="0" patch_number="6" project="chromiumos/third_party/autotest" project_url="https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/chromiumos/third_party/autotest" ref="refs/changes/07/494007/6" remote="cros" total_fail_count="0"/><lkgm version="9522.0.0-rc3"/></manifest>